[74.405, "o", "  \u001b[38;2;113;157;130m\u001b[1m[ASSIMILATE]\u001b[0m Recording research experience...\r\n"]
[0.693, "o", "\r\nPython 3.13, released on October 7, 2024, introduces several key features across the interpreter, data model, standard library, and typing system [1, 2].\r\n\r\n### Interpreter Improvements\r\n* **New Interactive Interpreter:** A new interactive shell is now the default, based on code from the PyPy project [1, 2]. Key features include:\r\n    * Multiline editing with history preservation [1, 2].\r\n    * Direct support for REPL-specific commands like `help`, `exit`, and `quit` without the need to call them as functions [1, 2].\r\n    * Color support for prompts and tracebacks enabled by default (can be controlled via `PYTHON_COLORS` or `NO_COLOR` environment variables) [1, 2].\r\n    * Interactive help browsing using `F1` with a separate command history [1, 2].\r\n    * History browsing using `F2` that skips output as well as the `>>>` and `...` prompts [1, 2].\r\n    * \"Paste mode\" using `F3` that makes pasting larger blocks of code easier [1, 2].\r\n* **Improved Error Messages:** \r\n    * Tracebacks are now highlighted in col"]
[0.301, "o", "or by default [1, 2].\r\n    * More helpful error messages are provided when a script has the same name as a standard library or third-party module [1, 2].\r\n    * The interpreter now suggests the correct keyword argument when an incorrect one is passed to a function [1, 2].\r\n* **Free-threaded CPython (Experimental):** Experimental support for running with the global interpreter lock (GIL) disabled [1, 2]. This allows threads to run in parallel on available CPU cores [1]. This mode requires a different executable (e.g., `python3.13t`) and is not enabled by default [1].\r\n* **Just-In-Time (JIT) Compiler (Experimental):** A basic JIT compiler was added via PEP 744, which is currently disabled by default [1, 2].\r\n\r\n### Python Data Model Improvements\r\n* `__static_attributes__`: Stores the names of attributes accessed through `self.X` in any function in a class body [1, 2].\r\n* `__firstlineno__`: Records the first line number of a class definition [1, 2].\r\n* `locals()`: The `locals()` builtin now has defined semantics "]
[0.301, "o", "for changing the returned mapping [1, 2].\r\n\r\n### Standard Library Improvements\r\n* **New Exceptions/Functions:**\r\n    * `PythonFinalizationError`: A new exception raised when an operation is blocked during finalization [1, 2].\r\n    * `copy.replace()`: A new function with support for many builtin types and any class defining the `__replace__()` method [1, 2].\r\n    * `base64.z85encode()` and `base64.z85decode()`: New functions that support encoding and decoding Z85 data [1, 2].\r\n* **Module Updates:**\r\n    * `argparse`: Now supports deprecating command-line options, positional arguments, and subcommands [1, 2].\r\n    * `dbm`: The new `dbm.sqlite3` module is now the default `dbm` backend [1, 2].\r\n    * `os`: Includes a suite of new functions for working with Linux's timer notification file descriptors [1, 2].\r\n    * `random`: Now has a command-line interface [1, 2].\r\n\r\n### New Typing Features\r\n* **Default Type Parameters (PEP 696):** Type parameters (`typing.TypeVar`, `typing.ParamSpec`, and `typing.TypeVarTuple`) "]
[0.302, "o", "now support default values [1, 2].\r\n* **Deprecation Marking (PEP 702):** The new `warnings.deprecated()` decorator adds support for marking deprecations in the type system and at runtime [1, 2].\r\n* **Read-only Items (PEP 705):** `typing.ReadOnly` can be used to mark an item of a `typing.TypedDict` as read-only for type checkers [1, 2].\r\n* **Type Narrowing (PEP 742):** `typing.TypeIs` provides more intuitive type narrowing behavior as an alternative to `typing.TypeGuard` [1, 2].\r\n\r\n### Platform Support\r\n* **Official Support:** Apple's iOS and Android are now officially supported at tier 3 [1, 2].\r\n* **WebAssembly:** `wasm32-wasi` is now supported as a tier 2 platform, while `wasm32-emscripten` is no longer an officially supported platform [1, 2].\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;2;112;143;181m\u001b[1mSources:\u001b[0m\r\n  [1] What\u2019s New In Python 3.13 \u2014 Python 3.14.7 documentation \u2014 https://docs.python.org/3/whatsnew/3.13.html\r\n"]
[0.301, "o", "  [2] cpython/Doc/whatsnew/3.13.rst at main \u00b7 python/cpython \u00b7 GitHub \u2014 https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/main/Doc/whatsnew/3.13.rst\r\n\u001b[38;2;112;143;181m\u001b[1mPDF report:\u001b[0m reports/2026-08-25_what-are-the-key-new-features-in-python-3-13_res_77539f434ef7.pdf\r\n\u001b[38;2;120;129;145m\u001b[2m\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u001b[0m\r\n  Steps: 7 | Type next question or 'exit'\r\n\u001b[38;2;120;129;145m\u001b[2m\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u001b[0m\r\n>>> "]
